1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,017
A turbulence dictionary is a short file: a simulation type, a model, two switches. Nothing in it says what the model actually is.

2
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:11,899
Select the model. RASModel is the old spelling — OpenFOAM reads 'model' now — and the pane says so rather than hiding it, because nearly every tutorial still writes it this way.

3
00:00:11,899 --> 00:00:20,099
And here is what kEpsilon is, in OpenFOAM's own words, quoted from the model's source header — with Launder and Spalding, the paper that defines it.

4
00:00:20,099 --> 00:00:25,399
Step down the list and every value brings its own. Where upstream cites no paper the note is left empty rather than filled with a guess.

5
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:30,399
Some are three papers rather than one.

6
00:00:30,399 --> 00:00:34,199
Sixteen models in this list, twenty-nine across RAS and LES.

7
00:00:34,199 --> 00:00:37,999
Every one of them read out of the source of seventeen OpenFOAM releases.

8
00:00:37,999 --> 00:00:42,899
Which is what the Supported In row is counting: measured, rather than assumed.

9
00:00:42,899 --> 00:00:49,899
And it is not a handful of them that carry this.

10
00:00:49,899 --> 00:00:56,399
The last row is the exception that proves it: laminar names no model class, so there is nothing upstream to quote — and that one sentence is FoDE's own.

11
00:00:56,399 --> 00:01:00,399
Nothing has been changed. The tree still reads kEpsilon and the file is not marked dirty: the value moves only when you press Apply Value. Reading the list is free.
